# How we made the essays

We began with one complete loop: an AI chose a question, read primary sources, drafted an essay, received criticism from a fresh context, and revised. The first ten essays showed why the process needed to evolve: separate authors still converged on similar themes. Later batches assigned broad areas before drafting, tested different kinds of criticism, and moved some rejection decisions ahead of the expensive writing stage.

The human chose the process, resources, style requests, and selection priorities. AI authors developed the philosophical content from their own assignments and materials; the coordinator retained the wider history.

**Key dates:** September 8 — first isolated essays, with no word target; September 10 — plain-language batch; September 22 — contribution check before drafting; September 25 — six-method expansion; October 10 — general clear-prose instruction. [Full chronology](timeline.md).

## The first hundred

| Batch | Completed essays | What changed |
| --- | ---: | --- |
| First pilot | 10 | One author–critic–revision cycle |
| Broader fields | 20 | Areas allocated in advance; lighter reasoning setting |
| Plain language | 20 | Explicit criticism of difficult prose as well as argument |
| Judge-guided writing | 8 | Two named profiles criticized each draft; 12 unstarted assignments were cancelled |
| Claude series | 10 | Five author-only essays; five with separate criticism and revision |
| Creative search | 10 | Seventeen attempts, with both completed and early rejections preserved |
| Speculative scenarios | 5 | Unusual thought experiments, written in Russian |
| Earlier Claude pilot | 1 | Completed through a recorded Codex length correction |
| Six-method extension | 16 | Free inquiry, tournament, debate, source studies, counterexamples, and revision branches |

The [100-essay collection](../essays/README.md) counts each completed line of development once. Earlier drafts and rejected ideas remain alongside it. The table also shows a deliberate difference in treatment: some essays had separate critics; others relied on self-review.

## Check the contribution before writing the essay

The creative series initially wrote full manuscripts before asking whether the contribution justified them. Its first three essays failed that check. From attempt four, a separate critic examined the proposal before drafting began. Across 17 attempts, three were rejected after writing and four before it; ten were accepted. The [seven unsuccessful attempts](../essays/README.md#set-aside-attempts) preserve both kinds of decision.

We also separated two questions: **does the argument work?** and **does it add enough?** An opponent and a contribution critic could challenge different weaknesses. Authors had to respond, but could defend a reasoned disagreement.

## Six ways to begin

| Method | Actual process |
| --- | --- |
| Free inquiry | Three investigations without a separate critic; self-review kept its own label |
| Idea tournament | Three generators supplied 40 ideas each; two duplicate ideas received fresh replacements. Two evaluators produced 240 assessments, followed by 120 → 24 → 6 → 3 selection |
| Debate | Two opening positions, three rounds of objections and replies, then a fresh third author; agreement was optional |
| Source expedition | Start from primary texts, including Zhuangzi, Cavendish and Hooke, and work toward a question |
| Counterexample laboratory | Use executable models for a specific claim; in one case a critic independently reproduced a search over 65,536 models |
| Revision branches | Preserve the same starting draft, then compare no revision, one cycle, and three cycles |

The tournament retained reasons for 96 early deferrals and reserved a place for an unusual disputed idea. The two duplicates remained in the history: 122 attempted sketches produced the 120 admitted proposals.

In the two branching cases, a blind selector preferred the one-cycle version once and the three-cycle version once. Because models and contexts also changed, this records two version choices rather than identifying an optimal number of revisions.

## Plain language, intact arguments

The 6,000-word ceiling left authors free to use the space their argument needed. The plain-language batch made clarity an explicit task; the later general instruction asked for simpler expression while retaining premises, objections, and qualifications.

A length correction showed why content needed its own check. While shortening [E076, the earlier Claude pilot](../essays/E076/README.md), an editor removed a qualification that limited the claim. A fresh critic caught the change. The qualification was restored, and twelve other cuts remained.

## What changed with resources

The corpus spans Astra, Sol, Luna, and Claude, with different reasoning settings and some mixed lineages. The hundred-essay campaign paused at 89 completed essays, then resumed under a different model setting, keeping the completed work.

Each biography records the roles, inputs, and observed settings needed to understand those changes. Unequal assignments and resources make these exploratory histories unsuitable for a controlled comparison of models. The [post-midterm account](post-midterm.md) continues the story with seven more completed essays.

